  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Q3 - Mean median and an unusually high age</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>What does a typical farmer's age look like?</h3><p>A small, illustrative survey records the ages of seven farmers. One respondent
is noticeably older than the others. The ages, in ascending order, are:</p><p><strong>{@a1@}, {@a2@}, {@a3@}, {@a4@}, {@a5@}, {@a6@}, {@older@}</strong></p><h4>A. Predict</h4><p>Before calculating, which statistic do you expect to be affected more by the
unusually high age? [[input:predict]] [[validation:predict]]
[[feedback:prtpredict]]</p><h4>B. Calculate with all seven farmers</h4><p>Calculate the <strong>mean age</strong> of the seven farmers.
Give your answer to two decimal places if necessary.
[[input:mean7]] [[validation:mean7]] [[feedback:prtmean7]]</p><p>Calculate the <strong>median age</strong> of the seven farmers.
[[input:median7]] [[validation:median7]] [[feedback:prtmedian7]]</p>
[[hint title="Hint: mean and median"]]
<p>The mean is the sum of the ages divided by the number of farmers.
For seven ages in ascending order, the median is the fourth age.</p>
[[/hint]]
<h4>C. Investigate the unusually high age</h4><p>For comparison, suppose we temporarily leave out the oldest farmer.
What is the <strong>mean age</strong> of the remaining six farmers?
[[input:mean6]] [[validation:mean6]] [[feedback:prtmean6]]</p><p>What is the <strong>median age</strong> of the remaining six farmers?
[[input:median6]] [[validation:median6]] [[feedback:prtmedian6]]</p><p>By how many years does including the oldest farmer <strong>increase the mean</strong>?
Give your answer to two decimal places if necessary.
[[input:meanincrease]] [[validation:meanincrease]]
[[feedback:prtmeanincrease]]</p><h4>D. Interpret</h4><p>Which statistic better describes the age of a typical farmer <em>among
the main group of six</em>, without being pulled upwards by the unusually
high value? [[input:typical]] [[validation:typical]]
[[feedback:prttypical]]</p><p>Which explanation best supports your choice?
[[input:explain]] [[validation:explain]] [[feedback:prtexplain]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked solution</h3><p>The seven ages are {@a1@}, {@a2@}, {@a3@}, {@a4@}, {@a5@},
{@a6@} and {@older@} years.</p><h4>With all seven farmers</h4><p>The mean is the sum of all seven ages divided by seven:</p>
\[
\bar{x}_7 =
\frac{{@a1@}+{@a2@}+{@a3@}+{@a4@}+{@a5@}+{@a6@}+{@older@}}{7}
= {@q_mean7@}.
\]
<p>The median is the fourth age in the ordered list:
<strong>{@q_median7@} years</strong>.</p><h4>Without the oldest farmer</h4><p>The mean of the remaining six ages is:</p>
\[
\bar{x}_6 =
\frac{{@a1@}+{@a2@}+{@a3@}+{@a4@}+{@a5@}+{@a6@}}{6}
= {@q_mean6@}.
\]
<p>With six ordered ages, the median is the mean of the third and
fourth ages:</p>
\[
\operatorname{median}_6
=\frac{{@a3@}+{@a4@}}{2}
={@q_median6@}.
\]
<p>Including the oldest farmer increases the mean by
\({@q_mean7@}-{@q_mean6@}={@q_meanincrease@}\) years.
The median changes by only
\({@q_median7@}-{@q_median6@}={@medianincrease@}\) year.</p><p>The mean uses every value and is pulled upwards by an unusually
high observation. The median depends on the middle position(s), so it is
less affected by the size of the oldest age. For describing the age of
a typical farmer in the main group of six, the median of the full
dataset is therefore useful. The older farmer is still a valid
observation and should not be deleted without a substantive reason.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
